body:
@zzzeek hey and thanks for the prompt and detailed response. I spent some more time on it today and was able to produce an MCVE as you suggested. This did help me pin down the issue to something to do with the usage of `from_self()` (simply removing the `from_self()` clause below will get rid of the issue):

```python
#!/usr/bin/env python
from sqlalchemy import Column, Integer, String, create_engine
from sqlalchemy.sql.functions import FunctionElement
from sqlalchemy.ext.compiler import compiles
from sqlalchemy.orm import declarative_base, sessionmaker


Base = declarative_base()


class User(Base):
     __tablename__ = 'users'

     id = Column(Integer, primary_key=True)
     name = Column(String)


class now(FunctionElement):
    name = "now"


@compiles(now)
def compile(element, compiler, **kwargs):
    raise NotImplementedError(f"this function is only defined for postgresql")


@compiles(now, "postgresql")
def compile_postgres(element, compiler, **kwargs):
    return "current_timestamp"


def go(engine):
    Session = sessionmaker(bind=engine)
    with engine.connect() as conn:
        session = Session()
        session.query(
            now(),
        ).from_self().all()


def main():
    engine = create_engine("postgresql://test@localhost/test_db", echo=True)
    Base.metadata.drop_all(engine)
    Base.metadata.create_all(engine)
    go(engine)


if __name__ == "__main__":
	main()
```

Some more info from my debugging

I *think* there is something along the way when using `from_self` which loses the current dialect as context. I was able to get one stack trace at some point using the more elaborate unit-tests I pointed to above which included the following snippet:

```
 File "/Users/adamever-hadani/.pyenv/versions/3.8.1/envs/microcosm-eventsource/lib/python3.8/site-packages/sqlalchemy/sql/elements.py", line 905, in _proxy_key
    return str(self)
  File "/Users/adamever-hadani/.pyenv/versions/3.8.1/envs/microcosm-eventsource/lib/python3.8/site-packages/sqlalchemy/sql/elements.py", line 572, in __str__
    return str(self.compile())
  File "/Users/adamever-hadani/.pyenv/versions/3.8.1/envs/microcosm-eventsource/lib/python3.8/site-packages/sqlalchemy/sql/elements.py", line 505, in compile
    return self._compiler(dialect, **kw)
  File "/Users/adamever-hadani/.pyenv/versions/3.8.1/envs/microcosm-eventsource/lib/python3.8/site-packages/sqlalchemy/sql/elements.py", line 568, in _compiler
    return dialect.statement_compiler(dialect, self, **kw)
```

In particular, using `str(self)` as the key in sqlalchemy.sql.elements (line 905 in _proxy_key per above stack trace), seems to end up with a call to `compile()` without passing any arguments, and in particular without passing the dialect.

The stack is quite elaborate so I would hope for some help tracing this back further.

Another note: I read the migration notes to sqlalchemy 2.x which suggest that `from_self` is deprecated in favor of `aliased`. I am able to reproduce the same issue when using aliased however, e.g.:

```python
#!/usr/bin/env python
from sqlalchemy import Column, Integer, String, create_engine
from sqlalchemy.sql.functions import FunctionElement
from sqlalchemy.ext.compiler import compiles
from sqlalchemy.orm import aliased, declarative_base, sessionmaker


Base = declarative_base()


class User(Base):
     __tablename__ = 'users'

     id = Column(Integer, primary_key=True)
     name = Column(String)


class now(FunctionElement):
    name = "now"


@compiles(now)
def compile(element, compiler, **kwargs):
    raise NotImplementedError(f"this function is only defined for postgresql")


@compiles(now, "postgresql")
def compile_postgres(element, compiler, **kwargs):
    return "current_timestamp"


def go(engine):
    Session = sessionmaker(bind=engine)
    with engine.connect() as conn:
        session = Session()
        subq = session.query(
            now(),
        ).subquery()

        alias = aliased(subq)

        return session.query(User, subq).all()


def main():
    engine = create_engine("postgresql://test@localhost/test_db", echo=True)
    Base.metadata.drop_all(engine)
    Base.metadata.create_all(engine)
    go(engine)


if __name__ == "__main__":
	main()
```

so...... we can just catch NotImplementedError also, or you could modify your recipe as follows:

```
from sqlalchemy import exc

@compiles(now)
def compile(element, compiler, **kwargs):
    raise exc.UnsupportedCompilationError(
        compiler, "now", f"this function is only defined for postgresql"
    )

```


what I might do is catch all exceptions here and then warn that UnsupportedCompilationError should be raised.
